{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:labor:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:78e293dbfb20b7309a451b43db340d15c56b67cb01b5014e15f18ee9744c6844","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1.2},{"probability":0.5,"value":2.5},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.9}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave 1 clusters tightly (2.0 / 2.1 / 2.2) and P2 found the one measurement error that matters: the locked 2.60% TIPS print is US-ONLY, and the G7 GDP-weighted object the question asks for is ~1.71-1.90% today. P1 explicitly anchored on ~1.90 and P3 on ~1.8-1.9, so all three are in fact forecasting a 20-40 bp RISE and calling it mean reversion. I go materially higher, to 2.5%, on three grounds Wave 1 either suppressed or got backwards. (1) MY OWN CQ3 FORCES IT, AND THIS IS A CONSISTENCY CONSTRAINT WAVE 1 ESCAPED BY FORECASTING LOW GROWTH. 2A locks global (r - g) worsening by ~+1.0 pp. P3 is the only seat that checks it, and he checks it the easy way: OECD g ~0.95 with r = 2.2 gives r-g ~ +1.25, which \"matches rather than contradicts\". But if g is higher — my OECD leg is 1.30 and coupled 2.1 — then a locked WORSENING of r-g means r must be higher still, not that r-g is satisfied more cheaply. A forecaster who raises g and leaves r alone has quietly improved r-g, which the locked prior forbids. 1.30 + 1.2 = 2.5. (2) THE FINANCIAL-REPRESSION WEDGE IS A REGIME ARTIFACT, NOT A STANDING CAPACITY. P2 books -0.40 pp G7-weighted with a -1.00 to 0.00 range, and P1 gives the repression base rate a high weight from the 1940s-50s and 2010s. Both periods share a feature absent in 2032-36: a central bank that WANTED duration — for war finance, then for a zero-bound reflation. A central bank running down a balance sheet into the duration supply that 2A locks (global gross debt >100% of GDP by 2029, ~142% US and ~127% China by 2031, net interest 2% -> 3% of GDP) is a seller, and LDI/Solvency II/LCR demand is a stock already substantially allocated, not a flow that scales with issuance. I book the wedge at -0.15, not -0.40. (3) THE JAPAN AND EURO LEGS ARE WHERE THE WEIGHTED MEASURE MOVES, AND ALL THREE UNDER-SIZED THEM. At ~0.11 Japan and ~0.10-0.11 Germany, a Japanese 10y real moving -0.20 -> +1.20 and a German 0.70 -> 1.60 is +15 bp and +10 bp weighted respectively; add the US leg not falling from 2.60 (2A gives no reason it should) and France/Italy/UK/Canada normalizing, and 2.5% is arithmetic rather than a call. DECOMPOSITION: r* 1.95 (base 1.15, + 0.15 life-cycle dissaving, + 0.45 public dissaving, + 0.25 eroding convenience yield — named directly in 2A — + 0.10 net AI/grid savings demand after the AI-Q4 haircut, - 0.15 precautionary saving) + real term premium 0.70 - repression 0.15 = 2.50. P90 3.9 is deliberately above every Wave-1 P90 (3.1 / 3.3 / 3.4) because the fiscal-dominance tail is the one the locked priors describe most concretely (3-yr P95 Debt-at-Risk ~117% of GDP, +2.4 pp under a 20% AI-led equity shock, basis-trade absorption replacing price-insensitive convenience-yield demand) and because that tail is convex — the term premium and the convenience-yield erosion compound. P10 1.2 holds the downside premortem: the capex bust plus BIS demand deficit plus a repression response, which is where my own numbers are most vulnerable. I take P2's directional reading off the simulation and accept his refusal of its level: a model whose rate moves +0.87 pp for fiscal/political stress and +0.09 pp for the strongest technology regime says to put the variance in the fiscal and term-premium terms, which is what the 2.7 pp span between my P10 and P90 does.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:55.972Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCas